This survey introduces the concept of Learning from Educator-Pupil Interactions (EPI), a human feedback mechanism complementary to standard teacher-based approaches (e.g., learning from human corrections or natural language interactions). We define an EPI as a sequence of interactions involving an educator and a pupil, where the educator observes the pupil's actions and decides whether to intervene. We outline the framework for EPI, detailing how knowledge graphs can be constructed via this process. Furthermore, we present a novel Educational-Pupil framework for acquiring knowledge graphs, which guides a knowledge graph (KG) from an initial macroscopic structure to a fully specified version through a series of steps. We categorize EPI methods into three groups: direct induction, meta-reasoning, and hybrid methods, offering a comprehensive overview of existing techniques for each category. Finally, we propose a set of open challenges and future research directions within the field of EPI. To support this, we also introduce a new Educational-Pupil framework for knowledge graph induction and define a corresponding metric for evaluating induced graphs. The accompanying code is accessible at https://github.com/Abhishek-1999/LEA-EPF.

