u_{\mu}$ and related semileptonic decay branching fractions are minimized. Furthermore, the method by which theoretical uncertainties associated with the form factors are reduced to facilitate precision tests of QCD is detailed. Utilizing the conventional basis of heavy meson effective theory (HQET) and $D_s^*$-pole models, the validity of the resulting differential decay rate is confirmed through comparisons with existing data under various theoretical frameworks. The findings indicate that both neutronic and $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ differential spectra exhibit significant non-Gaussian characteristics, whereas the distribution of $f_T$, defined as the ratio of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ to $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^0\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ differential decay rates, aligns well with Gaussian behavior. These results can assist experiments in optimizing the extraction of $g_{_{m PS}}(w)$ from the lepton-pair invariant mass spectrum when aiming to determine $f_2$ or related form factors. Additionally, this work provides a correction to a recent publication by the same group regarding the determination of $f_1$. The ultimate objective is to enhance the precision of $V_{cs}$ determinations derived from $B_s$ meson decays.
